  ex:l8-script-material-residual-orthogonality a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 6 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l8-orthogonality-section ; dct:title "Two residual conditions as one matrix equation, with complete symbol legends"@en ; dct:references ex:l8-f-orthogonality-input, ex:l8-f-orthogonal ; cd:relatedScene ex:l8-scene-residual-orthogonality ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l8-script-ch4 dct:references ex:l8-script-material-residual-orthogonality ; cd:hasScriptMaterial ex:l8-script-material-residual-orthogonality ; cd:relatedScene ex:l8-scene-residual-orthogonality .
  ex:l8-script-material-2-23 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 7 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l8-normal-from-residuals-section ; dct:title "Derive and solve the normal equation"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l8-script-ch2 a cd:ScriptSection ; cd:position 2 ; dct:title "Matrix algebra and simultaneous equations"@en ; cd:body "**Matrix Algebra**\n\nThis short detour introduces the operations we will use to express the two regression conditions together.\n\n**Matrix Shapes: count rows before columns**\n\nCompare the 2 by 3, 3 by 2 and 2 by 4 arrays. Count horizontal rows first and vertical columns second. The first two arrays contain the same number of entries, but their shapes differ.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-2-2]]\n**Matrix Indexing: row first, then column**\n\nRead a subscript pair in a fixed order. In the shown matrix, a subscript 23 means row 2 then column 3, so its value is 6. It does not mean row 3 column 2, and it does not mean the number twenty-three. The box marks that matrix entry.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-2-4]]\n**Matrix Types: square matrices**\n\nCompare the 2 by 2, 3 by 3 and 4 by 4 examples. Every one is square because its row and column counts match. The positions and values of the entries can vary.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-2-6]]\n**Matrix Types: diagonal matrices**\n\nCompare diagonal matrices of sizes 2, 3 and 4. The highlighted diagonal is where the row and column indices agree. Every off-diagonal entry is zero. The 3 by 3 example has a zero on its main diagonal, which makes it singular.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-2-7]]\n**Matrix Types: identity matrices**\n\nCompare the identities of sizes 1, 2 and 3. Every diagonal entry is one. The left identity for a 2 by 3 matrix must have size 2, and the right identity must have size 3. Both products preserve the same matrix A.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-2-8]]\n**Matrix Arithmetic: addition and subtraction**\n\nBoth matrices have the same shape. Read the top-left cells first: add 1 and 5 or subtract 5 from 1. Then do the same at each of the other three positions. The result keeps the original shape. When entries have units, the two values being combined must have compatible units.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-2-9]]\n**Matrix Arithmetic: multiplication by a scalar**\n\nMultiply each entry by the same scalar. The first calculation multiplies 1 by 2, and the remaining cells repeat the same operation with 2, 3 and 4. The positions and the shape stay the same. A dimensionless scalar preserves the units of the entries.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-2-10]]\n**Matrix Transpose: rows become columns**\n\nThe first row [1,2,3] becomes the first column and the second row [4,5,6] becomes the second column. Shape two-by-three therefore becomes three-by-two. Transposing twice returns the original matrix. Later X transpose will collect dot products with the columns of X.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-2-11]]\n**Matrix Multiplication: which dimensions must match?**\n\nA matrix with m rows and n columns can multiply one with n rows and p columns. Each output entry needs a complete row and a complete column of equal length. The result has m rows and p columns. As a quick question, a two-by-three times a three-by-four is valid and yields two-by-four; the reverse order is not valid. Pause for the shape answer before continuing.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-2-12]]\n**Matrix Multiplication: one row meets one column**\n\nEach row of A has three entries and each column of B has three entries. Start with the boxed row and column. Multiply the three matching pairs and add to get 5 in the top-left cell. The other three cells use the three remaining row-column pairs and give 13, 14 and 31. The result has two rows from A and two columns from B.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-2-14]]\n**Matrix Multiplication: why order matters**\n\nUse a new pair of two-by-two matrices so both products have the same dimensions. The order still matters: AB and BA have different entries. Noncommutativity is about the operation, not a rounding error. For rectangular matrices one order may not even exist. Do not rearrange matrix factors as though they were scalar numbers.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-2-16]]\n**Matrix Arithmetic: entrywise multiplication**\n\nHadamard multiplication combines corresponding entries of equally shaped matrices. For the same A and B it gives rows [5,12] and [21,32]. This is neither AB nor BA. In R, an asterisk is entrywise multiplication and percent-star-percent is matrix multiplication. Keep that distinction when reading model code.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-2-17]]\n**Matrix Inverse: undoing a matrix product**\n\nFor the new example A = [[2,1],[1,1]], the inverse is [[1,−1],[−1,2]]. Check either multiplication order: the diagonal products sum to one and off-diagonal products sum to zero. An inverse is not obtained by taking reciprocals of individual entries. The inverse exists only for nonsingular square matrices.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-2-19]]\n**Matrix Inverse: when no inverse exists**\n\nThe columns of [[1,2],[2,4]] are dependent: the second is twice the first. They can only generate one direction. Different coefficient pairs can produce the same output, so multiplication has lost the information needed to reverse it uniquely. The equation next to A shows the dependence directly.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-2-21]]\n**Matrix Equations: write two equations as one**\n\nThe equations 2u1 + u2 = 5 and u1 + u2 = 3 become Au = b with the same inverse example and b = [5,3]. Left-multiply by the inverse to obtain u = [2,1]. Substitute back: 2 times 2 plus 1 equals 5 and 2 plus 1 equals 3. Row equations and the matrix equation describe the same problem.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-2-22]]"@en ; cd:hasScriptMaterial ex:l8-script-material-2-2, ex:l8-script-material-2-4, ex:l8-script-material-2-6, ex:l8-script-material-2-7, ex:l8-script-material-2-8, ex:l8-script-material-2-9, ex:l8-script-material-2-10, ex:l8-script-material-2-11, ex:l8-script-material-2-12, ex:l8-script-material-2-14, ex:l8-script-material-2-16, ex:l8-script-material-2-17, ex:l8-script-material-2-19, ex:l8-script-material-2-21, ex:l8-script-material-2-22 ; dct:references ex:l8-script-material-2-2, ex:l8-script-material-2-4, ex:l8-script-material-2-6, ex:l8-script-material-2-7, ex:l8-script-material-2-8, ex:l8-script-material-2-9, ex:l8-script-material-2-10, ex:l8-script-material-2-11, ex:l8-script-material-2-12, ex:l8-script-material-2-14, ex:l8-script-material-2-16, ex:l8-script-material-2-17, ex:l8-script-material-2-19, ex:l8-script-material-2-21, ex:l8-script-material-2-22 ; cd:relatedScene ex:l8-scene-08, ex:l8-scene-09, ex:l8-scene-10, ex:l8-scene-12, ex:l8-scene-13, ex:l8-scene-14, ex:l8-scene-15, ex:l8-scene-16, ex:l8-scene-17, ex:l8-scene-18, ex:l8-scene-19, ex:l8-scene-20, ex:l8-scene-21, ex:l8-scene-22, ex:l8-scene-23, ex:l8-scene-24 ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l8-script-material-3-1 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 1 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l8-media-design-build ; dct:title "Design Matrix: build it from the measurements – figure"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l8-script-material-3-2 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 2 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l8-f-row ; dct:title "Design Matrix: one row per observation – formula and complete symbol legend"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l8-script-material-3-3 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 3 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l8-f-design ; dct:title "Design Matrix: one column per model term – formula and complete symbol legend"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l8-script-material-3-4 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 4 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l8-f-prediction ; dct:title "Matrix Prediction: multiply by the coefficient column – formula and complete symbol legend"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l8-script-material-3-5 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 5 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l8-f-units ; dct:title "Matrix Prediction: check the units – formula and complete symbol legend"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l8-script-ch3 a cd:ScriptSection ; cd:position 3 ; dct:title "From the total differential to the fitted line"@en ; cd:body "**Back to the Least-Squares Minimum**\n\nReturn from matrix algebra to the five detector readings. We know what to minimise. Now we have the tools to find the coefficients.\n\n**Least Squares: recall the total differential**\n\nWe return to the same five detector readings and the same line. Q is the squared-error score. When either coefficient changes, dQ describes the first-order change in that score. At the minimum, dQ is zero for every change of the two coefficients.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-minimum-two-coefficients-script-2]]\n\n**Least Squares: write out both derivatives**\n\nThe intercept subtracts one from every residual for each unit increase. The slope subtracts the measured time for each unit increase. The chain rule gives twice the residual times its derivative. Set each expression equal to zero.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-4-2]]\n\n**Least Squares: collect the coefficient terms**\n\nInsert the residual expression into the two zero sums. Expand each sum and move the intercept and slope terms to the left. The measured values stay on the right.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-4-5]]\n\n**Matrix Form: write the two regression equations as one**\n\nAs in the matrix algebra example, read the coefficients in each equation from left to right. Put them in one row of M. Keep the known values in order in the right-hand-side column r.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-4-6]]\n\n**Matrix Algebra: solve for the fitted coefficients**\n\nStart with M times the fitted coefficient column equals r. If M is invertible, multiply both sides on the left by its inverse. The inverse times M becomes I, so only the coefficient column remains on the left.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-4-1]]\n\n**Worked Example: put the measurements into the equation**\n\nReturn to the five detector readings. Add the times, their squares, the signals and the time–signal products. Put these sums into the coefficient matrix and the right-hand-side column. These are numerical coordinates in minutes and millivolts.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-1-1]]\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-4-8]]\n\n**Worked Example: find the inverse**\n\nCompute the determinant of M. It is 50 in the chosen numerical coordinates, so the inverse exists. Swap the two diagonal entries, negate the off-diagonal entries and divide by 50.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-4-9]]\n\n**Worked Example: calculate the intercept and slope**\n\nMultiply the inverse of M by r. The fitted intercept is 1.02 mV and the fitted slope is 1.99 mV/min. The fitted line predicts 5.995 mV at 2.5 minutes, answering the opening question.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-4-11]]\n\n**Return to the observations**\n\nThe fitted column is [1.02,3.01,5.00,6.99,8.98] mV. Subtracting it from the unchanged response column gives [0.08,−0.21,0.20,−0.09,0.02] mV. The signed sum and time-weighted sum are both zero to numerical precision, while the squared sum is 0.099 mV². This confirms the normal-equation calculation and distinguishes a fitted line from an exact interpolating curve.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-1-4]]\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-4-12]]"@en ; cd:hasScriptMaterial ex:l8-script-material-4-2, ex:l8-script-material-4-5, ex:l8-script-material-4-6, ex:l8-script-material-4-1, ex:l8-script-material-4-8, ex:l8-script-material-4-9, ex:l8-script-material-4-11, ex:l8-script-material-4-12 ; dct:references ex:l8-minimum-two-coefficients-script-2, ex:l8-script-material-4-2, ex:l8-script-material-4-5, ex:l8-script-material-4-6, ex:l8-script-material-4-1, ex:l8-script-material-1-1, ex:l8-script-material-4-8, ex:l8-script-material-4-9, ex:l8-script-material-4-11, ex:l8-script-material-1-4, ex:l8-script-material-4-12 ; cd:relatedScene ex:l8-scene-33, ex:l8-scene-34, ex:l8-scene-32, ex:l8-scene-35, ex:l8-scene-36, ex:l8-scene-31, ex:l8-scene-37, ex:l8-scene-38, ex:l8-scene-39, ex:l8-scene-40 ; cd:authoredResource true .
